Rule fixed before looking: CV <= ~10% -> CLAIM-012 stands as stated;
CV comparable to tau's 23-49% -> CLAIM-012 needs the same qualification.

================================================================================
VERDICT (2026-08-15), against the rule fixed before looking
================================================================================
CV = 25.5% (D=0) and 84.0% (D=0.02) -- comparable to or WORSE than tau's
23-49%. Time-averaging did NOT rescue the observable. At D=0.02 the four
realisations span 6.0 to 57.1, nearly a 10x range.

CONSEQUENCE for CLAIM-012: its reported percentages (88.6% / 108.3% / 73.0%
of the equipartition prediction) are single-trajectory draws from very wide
distributions and are WITHDRAWN as stated. The single D=0.02 trajectory
reported 46.2 (108%); the four-realisation mean here is 23.8 (55.8%).

WHAT SURVIVES, weakly: at D=0 the ensemble mean is 36.5 = 85.5% of the
prediction with SEM ~12.7% (n=4), i.e. consistent with equipartition within
about 1.1 sigma. That is a weak consistency statement, not the quantitative
agreement originally claimed.

WHAT DOES NOT: at D=0.02 the mean is 55.8% of prediction with CV 84% -- the
system has not equilibrated by T=128 (consistent with dispersion slowing
relaxation), so no equipartition comparison is meaningful there at these
horizons.

ALSO WITHDRAWN: CLAIM-012's "first-quarter means differ 4x across regulators
while late-time means converge" -- the basis for "the regulator's signature
is in the transient". With per-realisation CV of 25-84%, a 4x difference in
single-trajectory quarter-means is not distinguishable from noise.

The equipartition FORMULA itself, <Omega_sum>_eq = E(4^{N+1}-1)/(3(N+1)),
is analytic and unaffected. What fails is its empirical verification at the
precision claimed.
